WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.900
Film Courage: How did making your first feature
film and then getting feedback on that film

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.110
impact how you write things now?

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:09.470
Chris von Hoffmann, horror writer/director:
Oh hugely, massively because with making DRIFTER,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:17.009
I’m going to be very, very honest I was
not focusing that much on screenplay, on pedestrian

00:00:17.009 --> 00:00:18.099
narrative, on plotting, on character development.

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:23.470
I was very much focused on a cold exercise
in style.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:30.529
Very much making scene after scene of stuff
that I love and have a thin story stitched

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:36.840
together with duct tape, that was 100% the
intention of that movie.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:43.850
But I think we were getting a lot of feedback
when we were editing it and we knew and we

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:49.960
knew that there were some irreparable problems
that were dictated by the weakness of the

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:56.250
screenplay and kind of shot myself in the
foot a little bit by rushing into it because

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:03.420
people were…they were just taking the movie
so seriously they were being so film school

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:15.060
analytical about so many things because if
there is a story it’s one of those kind

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.499
of movies that start off as one thing and
by the end it’s a very much different thing,

00:01:18.499 --> 00:01:25.799
it keeps on transforming itself sort of like
this hybrid, genre mashup which is definitely

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:26.950
the movie I wanted to make at the time.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:32.729
I still think it’s a fun picture when I
watch it…”picture.”

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:36.630
I sound like I’m in the 40’s when I said
that.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:44.649
But with MONSTER PARTY, that was a really
wonderful experience because there is much

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:48.560
more pressure on that movie, I have a lot
of producers involved, a lot of great people

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.789
involved that have much more experience than
I am.

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:56.749
People who are extremely brutally honest from
the word go, from the writing stage, to the

00:01:56.749 --> 00:02:00.619
filming of it, to the editing of it, to the
sound, the music, everything.

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:04.000
I had people watching every element of that
movie.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.759
And they were all very strong collaborators,
I really love working with these guys.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:20.640
I think 
I was always wanting those heavy-hitting cut-throat

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.060
producers that were going to say “Chris,
this is not working.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.480
You have to fix this because you are going
to shoot yourself in the foot later on the

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.480
editing.”

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:38.120
And I had a lot of that on MONSTER PARTY,
because you get so inside the movie and you

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.030
need those outside perspectives and I didn’t
really have that much on DRIFTER because we

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:49.720
were all friends and we kind of all came up
together so I don’t think they were being

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.320
as harsh as they should have been.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.930
With MONSTER PARTY that was not the case,
but I think that was right in the long run

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.680
because at the end of the day it’s the movie.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:10.370
It’s not your auteur personality, it’s
not your cool stylist, it’s the movie.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:15.040
I mean at a certain point it’s not even
your movie anymore, it has to be as accessible

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:22.951
as possible and MONSTER PARTY was a great
transition for me, it was almost like a new

00:03:22.951 --> 00:03:33.030
chapter in my life to really explore new territory
and go back to the roots of the storytelling

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:42.530
I was attracted to and approach it from a
much more mature angle and can I have the

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:49.120
soundtrack and the style organically climb
inside the movie a little bit and not hijack

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.340
the movie like DRIFTER .

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:59.200
I’m very proud of the experience with MONSTER
PARTY, very proud of the movie.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.900
Film Courage: Do you think most of your characters…let’s
say any characters in any of your features

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.160
do they go through the hero’s journey.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.850
Because in some ways it’s different, a horror
movie is not going to end in a great light

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:19.340
where this hero has realized their potential
or saved something and it’s often not the

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:20.340
case.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.510
Chris: With DRIFTER I was trying to do that.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:32.070
But MONSTER PARY I would definitely say has
a hero’s journey.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:40.300
But I am very attracted to stories that are
populated by, I like morality tales.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.850
I like movies that have a moral tightrope
that the characters are walking that the lead

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:50.071
character might do something that you might
not agree with and it might be a little bit

00:04:50.071 --> 00:04:58.250
messed up and I’m going to challenge you
if you still want to go on the ride with him.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:03.980
A really random example that I bring up because
it’s so notorious for being so bad it’s

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.190
good which I think it a really genuinely fun
movie is SHOWGIRLS, which I love SHOWGIRLS,

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.190
it’s great.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:11.100
I’m not saying that ironically, I really
love that movie.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.950
The first time I saw it was with my Mother,
it was a very random, weird experience.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:20.380
But what’s really fascinating about that
movie and what I’m trying to do and what

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:26.870
I’m trying to do with MONSTER PARTY a lot
was every character in that movie was kind

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.860
of like has badness in them.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:33.060
I mean they might disguise it but they are
all kind of very…they have bad, bad qualities

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.610
about them, even Nomi (the main character
played by Elizabeth Berkley) and the only

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:41.980
innocent character in that movie is the girl
who is the make-up artist and she gets punished

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.620
for being innocent in this world of horrible
people.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.400
She gets punished quite horribly and people
forget about that really horrible scene in

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.050
the third act with her and the rock star guy.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:55.610
That’s a very eye-opening, vicious scene
where she gets punished for being good, a

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:01.480
good pure person around these terrible people
including the lead character.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:09.790
That’s sort of what I tried to dabble in
with monster party where its very flawed people

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:13.950
against even more flawed people and the people
you think would not be flawed were way more

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:18.551
flawed than you possibly could imagine but
there are a couple very innocent people in

00:06:18.551 --> 00:06:31.360
the movie who get extremely punished for being
very genuine.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:39.400
It sounds like a sick way of looking at things,
that’s always been sort of the outlook I

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:46.699
saw the world in where sometimes good deeds
go, like they always say “No good deed goes

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:48.169
unpunished” or whatever.

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:55.340
And innocent people paying the price for being
what they are and bad people having this power

00:06:55.340 --> 00:07:04.650
and the moral corruption of the world in our
society and our generation and I’m always

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:11.400
trying to make some sort of bold statement
about how I see, I’m not just trying to

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:17.690
make sort of cool genre movies and violence
and cool stuff.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:27.180
That’s all fun but I am trying to sneak
in a very bold statement about how I see the

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.860
world because a lot of it is very personal.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:34.210
As whacked out as some of the stuff is it
is very, very personal about the kind of tone

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:45.240
and the kind of nature of certain lead characters
that we’re supposed to be rooting for.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.200
I think when you’re writing stories you
have put all of your good traits on screen

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:52.400
but you also have to put a lot of your bad
traits on screen and no one wants to see just

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.400
good traits.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:08:01.210
You almost embarrass yourself by putting in
really bad traits about yourself.

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:09.090
Do it in a very fun, kind of accessible way
but those kind of stories I’ve always been

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.440
kind of attracted to.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:12.419
Film Courage: Or CARRIE.

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:14.130
Chris: Yes, CARRIE is great.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:18.570
Film Courage: Being punished at home, being
punished at high school.

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:28.750
Chris: That’s such a cynical story and then
she has no other options but to terrorize

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.050
her prom and then she pays the price for it.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:39.860
I mean that’s such a sad, great morality
tale.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:44.450
Because everyone has flaws and everyone has
darkness inside of them, some people do a

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:48.110
better job disguising them.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:09:04.790
But I like movies that really push the boundaries
of what our protagonist are capable of and

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.980
somewhat bad people against worst people.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:11.480
I’ve just always been attracted to that
kind of storytelling.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:18.760
Film Courage: Right and when you see the bad
people are the ones that are the stereotypical

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:22.020
ones that if you saw them in a dark alley
[you’d go the other way] and sometimes those

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:29.060
aren’t the ones that are the worst, it’s
the ones whom you would think are a little

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:30.060
more trustworthy.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.060
Chris:&nbsp; Yes.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:37.140
One of my favorite directors is Larry Clark
and films like BULLY and KIDS and KENT PARK,

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:44.491
those films are very disturbing experiences
but they are characters that…I grew up around

00:09:44.491 --> 00:09:46.050
people very much around those kind of people.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:51.620
My brother was very friends with those kind
of kids.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:57.870
So I think a lot of that kind of stuff, hearing
very revolting things that these kids would

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.880
say and seeing stuff that they would do and
hearing stories about them (what they would

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:13.310
do) and I think a lot of that stuff I try
to translate in a cinematic format and go

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:21.490
back to that…It just comes from growing
up and the kind of people I was around in

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:33.390
high school and the things that I saw and
just really disturbing, tragic things that

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:40.740
were happening with people and at a certain
point you just can’t quite see the world

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:50.940
any other ways until you get into your later
years but I’m just stemming about how the

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.700
world was around me growing up, the kind of
people that I interacted with, and the kind

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:59.750
that I grew up around and the people’s houses
that I went to and the kind of stuff I saw

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:04.210
and the really whacked out, dysfunctional
things that were happening inside these houses

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:08.220
and these kids.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:17.570
I don’t know, I’m just very attracted
to that kind of stuff and making it as grounded

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:21.960
as possible in a cinematic kind of way.

